' ============================================================================
' HIGH SCORE TABLE - DATA Statement Demo
' Written in VisualGasic for Godot 4.5+
' ============================================================================
' Demonstrates classic DATA/READ/RESTORE statements for embedding data
' ============================================================================
' VB6 module attribute — sets this file's class/module name inside the engine.
' Godot will register the node type under this name.
Attribute VB_Name = "HighScoreDemo"

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' PARALLEL ARRAYS — Classic BASIC data storage pattern
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Instead of a struct/class, classic VB6/BASIC stores related fields in
' separate arrays indexed by the same subscript. Entry i is:
'   playerNames(i), playerScores(i), playerDates(i)
' This mirrors how DATA/READ naturally fills arrays one field at a time.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Dim playerNames(10) As String    ' 3-letter arcade-style initials (e.g. "ACE")
Dim playerScores(10) As Integer  ' Score value — Integer can hold up to ~2 billion
Dim playerDates(10) As String    ' Date stamp or difficulty tag per entry
Dim scoreCount As Integer        ' How many entries are actually populated (0-10)

' Animation state for the blinking "INSERT COIN" text at the bottom
Dim blinkTimer As Single   ' Accumulates delta time; resets every 0.5 seconds
Dim showCursor As Boolean  ' Toggled each half-second to flash the text on/off

' ============================================================================
' INITIALIZATION  (Godot lifecycle callback)
' ============================================================================
' _Ready() is called once when the node enters the scene tree — the Godot
' equivalent of Form_Load in VB6. All one-time setup goes here.
Sub _Ready()
	Print "=== HIGH SCORE TABLE DEMO ==="
	Print "Demonstrating DATA, READ, and RESTORE statements"
	Print ""
	
	' Populate the parallel arrays from the embedded DATA section, then
	' dump them to the Output console for quick verification.
	LoadDefaultScores
	PrintScoresToConsole
End Sub

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' LoadDefaultScores — THE main DATA / READ / RESTORE showcase
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' HOW IT WORKS:
'   1. RESTORE <label>  — moves the internal "data pointer" to a named
'      DATA section anywhere in the file. Multiple labels let you keep
'      several data tables (DefaultHighScores, EasyDifficulty, etc.)
'   2. READ var          — pulls the next comma-separated value from the
'      current DATA line and advances the pointer. Types are converted
'      automatically (String stays String, numbers become Integer/Single).
'   3. Sentinel pattern  — the last DATA line contains "END" so the
'      reading loop knows when to stop (classic BASIC idiom).
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Sub LoadDefaultScores()
	' RESTORE positions the read pointer at the label "DefaultHighScores:"
	' (defined near the bottom of this file). Without this, Read would
	' continue from wherever the pointer was last left.
	Restore DefaultHighScores
	
	scoreCount = 0  ' Reset the live entry count before loading
	
	' Temporary variables to hold each field as it is READ from DATA lines.
	Dim playerName As String
	Dim score As Integer
	Dim dateStr As String
	
	' Do ... Loop (no condition) runs forever until Exit Do is hit.
	' This is the idiomatic VB6/VisualGasic way to read DATA with a sentinel.
	Do
		' READ one value — the first field on each DATA line is the name.
		Read playerName
		
		' Sentinel check: "END" signals no more real data to read.
		If playerName = "END" Then Exit Do
		
		' READ supports a comma-separated list to grab multiple values at
		' once. Here we pull the score (Integer) and date (String) together.
		Read score, dateStr
		
		' Store the three fields in their respective parallel arrays at the
		' same index — this keeps them linked by position.
		playerNames(scoreCount) = playerName
		playerScores(scoreCount) = score
		playerDates(scoreCount) = dateStr
		scoreCount = scoreCount + 1  ' Advance the live count
		
		' Safety guard: never exceed the declared array size (10).
		If scoreCount >= 10 Then Exit Do
	Loop
	
	Print "Loaded " & Str(scoreCount) & " high scores from DATA statements"
End Sub

' Print a nicely-aligned table to Godot's Output console (for debugging).
' Uses PadRight / PadLeft to create fixed-width columns — the same
' technique used in classic BBS / DOS score displays.
Sub PrintScoresToConsole()
	Print ""
	Print "=== CURRENT HIGH SCORES ==="
	
	Dim i As Integer
	For i = 0 To scoreCount - 1
		' Str() converts Integer to String; PadRight pads the name to 10
		' chars so columns stay aligned; PadLeft right-justifies the score.
		Print Str(i + 1) & ". " & PadRight(playerNames(i), 10) & " " & PadLeft(Str(playerScores(i)), 8) & "  " & playerDates(i)
	Next
	
	Print ""
End Sub

' ============================================================================
' HELPER FUNCTIONS — String formatting utilities
' ============================================================================
' PadRight / PadLeft replicate VB6's fixed-width formatting. They are used
' both for the console printout and for DrawString rendering so that columns
' line up regardless of the text length.
' ============================================================================

' PadRight: Appends spaces to the RIGHT of `text` until it reaches `length`.
' Example: PadRight("ACE", 10) → "ACE       " — left-justifies the text.
Function PadRight(text As String, length As Integer) As String
	Dim result As String = text        ' Start with the original text
	Do While Len(result) < length      ' Keep going until we hit the target width
		result = result & " "           ' Append one space per iteration
	Loop
	Return result                       ' Return the padded string
End Function

' PadLeft: Prepends spaces to the LEFT of `text` until it reaches `length`.
' Example: PadLeft("1000000", 8) → " 1000000" — right-justifies the text.
' Perfect for numeric columns where you want digits to align on the right.
Function PadLeft(text As String, length As Integer) As String
	Dim result As String = text        ' Start with the original text
	Do While Len(result) < length      ' Keep going until we hit the target width
		result = " " & result           ' Prepend one space per iteration
	Loop
	Return result                       ' Return the padded string
End Function

' ============================================================================
' GAME LOOP  (Godot lifecycle callback — called every frame)
' ============================================================================
' _Process(delta) is the Godot equivalent of a Timer event firing every
' frame. `delta` (Single) is the elapsed time in seconds since the last
' frame, which keeps animation speed independent of framerate.
Sub _Process(delta As Single)
	' Accumulate elapsed time to create a 0.5-second toggle interval.
	blinkTimer = blinkTimer + delta
	If blinkTimer >= 0.5 Then
		blinkTimer = 0                 ' Reset the timer for the next half-second
		showCursor = Not showCursor    ' Toggle Boolean — classic VB6 blink idiom
	End If
End Sub

' ============================================================================
' RENDERING  (Godot lifecycle callback — called every frame after _Process)
' ============================================================================
' _Draw() maps to Godot's _draw() override. All DrawRect / DrawString calls
' use VisualGasic wrappers around Godot's CanvasItem drawing API, giving an
' immediate-mode feel similar to classic arcade rendering loops.
' Color("#hex") creates a Godot Color from a CSS-style hex string.
Sub _Draw()
	' Fill the entire 640×480 viewport with a dark navy — classic CRT look.
	' DrawRect x, y, width, height, color
	DrawRect 0, 0, 640, 480, Color("#000033")
	
	' Title rendered in bright yellow at font size 36 — arcade attract mode style.
	' DrawString text, x, y, color, fontSize
	DrawString "HIGH SCORES", 220, 30, Color("#FFFF00"), 36
	
	' Column headers in muted gray — they label the four data columns.
	DrawString "RANK", 80, 90, Color("#888888"), 20
	DrawString "NAME", 160, 90, Color("#888888"), 20
	DrawString "SCORE", 320, 90, Color("#888888"), 20
	DrawString "DATE", 480, 90, Color("#888888"), 20
	
	' Thin horizontal rule (2 px tall rect) separating headers from entries.
	DrawRect 60, 115, 520, 2, Color("#444488")
	
	' --- Score entries (iterates over the parallel arrays) ---
	Dim i As Integer          ' Loop counter / array index
	Dim y As Integer          ' Computed vertical position for this row
	Dim rankColor As Variant  ' Variant holds the Color object for this rank
	
	For i = 0 To scoreCount - 1
		y = 130 + i * 30  ' Each row is 30 px apart, starting at y=130
		
		' Select Case (VB6 equivalent of switch) picks a color by rank.
		' Gold / Silver / Bronze for the podium; plain white for the rest.
		Select Case i
			Case 0
				rankColor = Color("#FFD700")  ' Gold   — 1st place
			Case 1
				rankColor = Color("#C0C0C0")  ' Silver — 2nd place
			Case 2
				rankColor = Color("#CD7F32")  ' Bronze — 3rd place
			Case Else
				rankColor = Color("#FFFFFF")  ' White  — everyone else
		End Select
		
		' Rank number ("1.", "2.", etc.)
		DrawString Str(i + 1) & ".", 90, y, rankColor, 24
		
		' Player initials from playerNames() — 3-letter arcade tradition
		DrawString playerNames(i), 160, y, rankColor, 24
		
		' Score, right-justified via PadLeft so digits align on the right edge.
		DrawString PadLeft(Str(playerScores(i)), 8), 300, y, rankColor, 24
		
		' Date/tag from playerDates() — rendered smaller and in gray.
		DrawString playerDates(i), 460, y, Color("#888888"), 20
	Next
	
	' Bottom divider — mirrors the top rule for visual symmetry.
	DrawRect 60, 430, 520, 2, Color("#444488")
	
	' Blinking "INSERT COIN" — showCursor is toggled in _Process every 0.5s.
	' When False the text simply isn't drawn, creating the classic blink.
	If showCursor Then
		DrawString "INSERT COIN", 250, 450, Color("#00FF00"), 24
	End If
	
	' Static credit counter — always visible in the lower-right corner.
	DrawString "CREDITS: 0", 500, 450, Color("#FFFFFF"), 16
End Sub

' ============================================================================
' EMBEDDED DATA — Classic BASIC-style inline data tables
' ============================================================================
' DATA lines store literal values directly inside the source code — no
' external CSV, JSON, or resource file is needed. The runtime reads them
' sequentially via the READ statement.
'
' KEY CONCEPTS:
'   • Labels (e.g. DefaultHighScores:) act as named bookmarks. You jump to
'     any label with RESTORE <label>, then READ from that point onward.
'   • Each DATA line holds a comma-separated list of mixed types:
'       "ACE"  → String     1000000 → Integer     "2026-01-15" → String
'     READ automatically converts values to the target variable's type.
'   • The sentinel line  Data "END", 0, ""  marks the end of a table so
'     the reading loop knows when to stop (see LoadDefaultScores above).
'   • Multiple labeled sections let you pack several independent tables
'     in one file — switch between them at runtime with RESTORE.
' ============================================================================

' --- Default top-10 table (loaded in _Ready via LoadDefaultScores) ----------
' RESTORE DefaultHighScores positions the read pointer HERE.
DefaultHighScores:
Data "ACE", 1000000, "2026-01-15"   ' #1  — name, score, date (3 fields per entry)
Data "MAX", 875000, "2026-01-14"    ' #2
Data "ZAP", 750000, "2026-01-13"    ' #3
Data "PRO", 625000, "2026-01-12"    ' #4
Data "DOC", 500000, "2026-01-11"    ' #5
Data "REX", 375000, "2026-01-10"    ' #6
Data "KID", 250000, "2026-01-09"    ' #7
Data "NEW", 125000, "2026-01-08"    ' #8
Data "ONE", 50000, "2026-01-07"     ' #9
Data "TRY", 25000, "2026-01-06"     ' #10
Data "END", 0, ""                   ' Sentinel — signals end of this data section

' --- Easy difficulty table ------------------------------------------------
' You could load this instead by calling: Restore EasyDifficulty
' before entering the READ loop — no code changes needed, just a
' different RESTORE target.
EasyDifficulty:
Data "AAA", 100000, "EASY"
Data "BBB", 75000, "EASY"
Data "CCC", 50000, "EASY"
Data "END", 0, ""                   ' Sentinel

' --- Hard difficulty table ------------------------------------------------
' Demonstrates that DATA sections can live anywhere in the file and
' contain any number of entries. RESTORE HardDifficulty jumps here.
HardDifficulty:
Data "GOD", 5000000, "HARD"
Data "PRO", 2500000, "HARD"
Data "VET", 1000000, "HARD"
Data "END", 0, ""                   ' Sentinel
